{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1468 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "f244f2f09eef3a5598837e14e221d9bd45e08d1b90c230fa5da8e4ad07ac837d" :edb/db-before-t 1467 :edb/db-after-t 1468 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534780 50 #inst "2026-10-12T12:42:45.543Z" 13194139534780 17592186045417 true] [17592186124194 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n![](images/06/06aff1dae72b170f576a5f2840bed4c57d199b429ec3bf2cc83c6754566bd06c.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, y \\geq -1, \\: y \\neq -x \\big\\}.\n$$" 13194139534780 17592186045417 false] [17592186124194 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n![](images/5b/5b534c95f0105645223836a1b44f38db3f14bf61425b6f53ee7815742a86a068.svg)\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, y \\geq -1, \\: y \\neq -x \\big\\}.\n$$" 13194139534780 17592186045417 true] [17592186124195 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n![](images/68/68197b31685c209b98416f8e137dca514d7fbbf382d400688d411fa633ffd3c0.svg)\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD = \\left \\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\, : \\, y \\gt -x, \\, y \\neq 1-x \\right \\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we plot the corresponding domain. \n\n- Starting with $y> -x,$ we draw a dashed line $y=-x$ and shade the region *above* the line.\n\n- Then, we draw a dashed line $y=1-x$ through the shaded region.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/6a/6aa1aaeadb177cf5a80bcb12a247725cb3396586e7516c3ba30b46b03a9d2612.png)" 13194139534780 17592186045417 true] [17592186124195 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n![](images/ca/caea647efe2231b9293d9f813772c79929229dbc472a2c04c4fb455a343473b5.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD = \\left \\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\, : \\, y \\gt -x, \\, y \\neq 1-x \\right \\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we plot the corresponding domain. \n\n- Starting with $y> -x,$ we draw a dashed line $y=-x$ and shade the region *above* the line.\n\n- Then, we draw a dashed line $y=1-x$ through the shaded region.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/6a/6aa1aaeadb177cf5a80bcb12a247725cb3396586e7516c3ba30b46b03a9d2612.png)" 13194139534780 17592186045417 false] [17592186124198 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive,\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y>0 \\\\ x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\big\\{(x, y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, 2x+y >0, \\: x > 0 \\big\\}.\n$$" 13194139534780 17592186045417 true] [17592186124198 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive,\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+y>0 \\\\ x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\big\\{(x, y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, 2x+y >0, \\: x > 0 \\big\\}.\n$$" 13194139534780 17592186045417 false] [17592186124636 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435808 13194139534780 17592186045417 true] [17592186124636 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435809 13194139534780 17592186045417 true] [17592186124638 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435810 13194139534780 17592186045417 true] [17592186124640 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435806 13194139534780 17592186045417 true] [17592186124640 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435807 13194139534780 17592186045417 true] [17592186124701 1050 540.0 13194139534780 17592186045417 true] [17592186124800 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves the $\\ge $ sign, the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ is part of the domain. Therefore, we draw a solid curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and shade the area *outside* the curve.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/ba/ba31bbc16c58860a4bc3c4d33ea1a61cb3d86d0e73d8aa6a67869b3e96a7fe32.png)" 13194139534780 17592186045417 true] [17592186124800 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1}.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves the $\\ge $ sign, the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ is part of the domain. Therefore, we draw a solid curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and shade the area *outside* the curve.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/ba/ba31bbc16c58860a4bc3c4d33ea1a61cb3d86d0e73d8aa6a67869b3e96a7fe32.png)" 13194139534780 17592186045417 false] [17592186125865 1064 "Another way of visualizing a two-variable function is to draw its graph as a **surface** in a three-dimensional coordinate system.\n\nFor example, consider the function\n\n$$z = f(x,y) = x^2 +y^2, \\quad (x,y)\\in D$$\n\nwhere the (restricted) domain is given by the unit disc\n\n$$D = \\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\: : \\: x^2+y^2 \\leq 1 \\big\\}.$$\n\nThe graph of $f$ is a surface that consists of all the points with coordinates\n\n$$(x, \\, y, \\, f(x,y)), \\quad (x,y) \\in D,$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3c9c7eca49a0f828e53c0775afac16335033c1a8881367e49098bf7125ee2e6.png)\n\nNotice that range of $f$ is represented by the interval $R=[0,1]$ on the vertical $z$-axis. It consists of the possible $z$-coordinates of the points on our surface." 13194139534780 17592186045417 false] [17592186125865 1064 "Another way of visualizing a two-variable function is to draw its graph as a **surface** in a three-dimensional coordinate system. \n\nFor example, consider the function\n\n$$\nz = f(x,y) = x^2 +y^2, \\quad (x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the (restricted) domain is given by the unit disc \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\: : \\: x^2+y^2 \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $f$ is a surface that consists of all the points with coordinates\n\n$$\n(x, \\, y, \\, f(x,y)), \\quad (x,y) \\in D,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3c9c7eca49a0f828e53c0775afac16335033c1a8881367e49098bf7125ee2e6.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that range of $f$ is represented by the interval $R=[0,1]$ on the vertical $z$-axis. It consists of the possible $z$-coordinates of the points on our surface." 13194139534780 17592186045417 true] [17592186125868 1064 "A function $f(x,y)$ of two variables is a rule that maps each pair of numbers $(x,y)\\in D$ to a unique real number. Under this notation, $D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2$ is the **domain** of $f.$\n\nFor instance, consider the following two-variable function: \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\sin(xy)}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this function by substituting concrete pairs of numbers for $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n- Let's evaluate $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2, \\pi).$ To do that, we substitute $x=2$ and $y=\\pi$ into the function expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,\\pi ) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(xy)}{\\sqrt{x-1}}{|}_{(2,\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\pi )}{\\sqrt{2-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(2,\\pi)$ returned a unique real number, this means that $(2,\\pi)$ belongs to the domain of $f,$ i.e., $(2,\\pi)\\in D.$\n\n- Let's now try to evaluate $f(x,y)$ at the point $(0, \\pi).$ To do that, we substitute $x=0$ and $y=\\pi$ into the function expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,\\pi ) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(xy)}{\\sqrt{x-1}}{|}_{(0,\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(0\\cdot \\pi )}{\\sqrt{0-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined over the real numbers.\n\nSince $f(0,\\pi)$ did not return a unique *real* number, this means that $(0,\\pi)\\notin D.$\n\nWe can also have functions of three variables $f(x,y,z),$ functions of four variables, $f(x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4),$ etc. There is no limit to the number of variables a function can have. Functions with more than one variable are called **multivariable functions.**" 13194139534780 17592186045417 false] [17592186125868 1064 "A function $f(x,y)$ of two variables is a rule that maps each pair of numbers $(x,y)\\in D$ to a unique real number. Under this notation, $D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2$ is the **domain** of $f.$\n\nFor instance, consider the following two-variable function: \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\sin(xy)}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this function by substituting concrete pairs of numbers for $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n- Let's evaluate $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2, \\pi).$ To do that, we substitute $x=2$ and $y=\\pi$ into the function expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,\\pi ) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{\\sqrt{x-1}}{|}_{(2,\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\pi )}{\\sqrt{2-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(2,\\pi)$ returned a unique real number, this means that $(2,\\pi)$ belongs to the domain of $f,$ i.e., $(2,\\pi)\\in D.$\n\n- Let's now try to evaluate $f(x,y)$ at the point $(0, \\pi).$ To do that, we substitute $x=0$ and $y=\\pi$ into the function expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,\\pi ) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{\\sqrt{x-1}}{|}_{(0,\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0\\cdot \\pi )}{\\sqrt{0-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined over the real numbers.\n\nSince $f(0,\\pi)$ did not return a unique *real* number, this means that $(0,\\pi)\\notin D.$\n\nWe can also have functions of three variables $f(x,y,z),$ functions of four variables, $f(x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4),$ etc. There is no limit to the number of variables a function can have. Functions with more than one variable are called **multivariable functions.**" 13194139534780 17592186045417 true] [17592186388995 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,(x.y)\\ne (0,0)\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $\\ne $ sign, the point $(0,0)$ is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we exclude the point $(x,y)=(0,0)$ and shade the plane.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/70/701c54bff1c431883f21cb980ac43f4f2f6beabb11817066a4e909d5a2663a25.png)" 13194139534780 17592186045417 true] [17592186388995 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,(x.y)\\ne (0,0)}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $\\ne $ sign, the point $(0,0)$ is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we exclude the point $(x,y)=(0,0)$ and shade the plane.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/70/701c54bff1c431883f21cb980ac43f4f2f6beabb11817066a4e909d5a2663a25.png)" 13194139534780 17592186045417 false] [17592186435776 1080 "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{3}\\ne -1\\}" 13194139534780 17592186045417 true] [17592186435776 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534780 17592186045417 true] [17592186435776 1083 #uuid "00ed170e-f9ef-581a-a28f-e895638d17f2" 13194139534780 17592186045417 true] [17592186435777 1080 "-3" 13194139534780 17592186045417 true] [17592186435777 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534780 17592186045417 true] [17592186435777 1083 #uuid "1c9017c3-0305-5a1f-91b7-5ff0abefd2b7" 13194139534780 17592186045417 true] [17592186435778 1080 "The domain is the entire $xy$-plane" 13194139534780 17592186045417 true] [17592186435778 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534780 17592186045417 true] [17592186435778 1083 #uuid "43ccfe2f-0560-528d-ba84-ade4a43c6a21" 13194139534780 17592186045417 true] [17592186435779 1080 "2\\sqrt{3}" 13194139534780 17592186045417 true] [17592186435779 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534780 17592186045417 true] [17592186435779 1083 #uuid "4cdfbe0f-7325-5f77-a8c6-761b1ce65557" 13194139534780 1759218